  card="The nineteenth century saw vast leaps in mass literacy in Europe, bringing in large numbers of new readers among children, women and workers. As primary education became compulsory from the late nineteenth century, children became an important category of readers and the production of school textbooks became critical for publishers. A children's press was set up in France in 1857. The Grimm Brothers compiled folk tales from peasants and edited them before publishing in 1812, leaving out anything unsuitable for children or vulgar to the elites, so print recorded old tales but also changed them. Women became important as readers and writers: penny magazines and manuals on behaviour and housekeeping were meant for them, and novelists such as Jane Austen, the Bronte sisters and George Eliot defined a new type of woman with will, strength of personality and the power to think. Lending libraries in England educated white-collar workers, artisans and lower-middle-class people, and after the working day was shortened from the mid-nineteenth century workers had time for self-improvement and wrote political tracts and autobiographies.",
  hint="Which new groups of readers emerged, and how did print change old folk tales?",
  probe="Who were the new readers of the nineteenth century and what did they read?",
  chain="Compulsory primary education made children readers, and a children's press and textbooks grew -> women became readers and novelists, defining a new type of woman -> shorter working days and lending libraries let workers read and write for self-improvement",
  links=[("education_children", "Connects primary education becoming compulsory to children becoming an important category of readers and the critical production of school textbooks.", ["nin_children"], True),
         ("shorter_day_workers", "Connects the gradually shortened working day to workers having time for self-improvement and self-expression.", ["nin_workers"], True)],
